<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Shoot-out at Package One, 1952</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;The United Nations vs. North Korea, 2 October 1952&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;I am&amp;nbsp;very grateful to author Edward C. Meyers and his book &lt;EM&gt;Thunder in the Morning Calm: The Royal Canadian Navy in Korea 1950-1955. &lt;/EM&gt;This scenario was inspired by Chapter-16 which relates to events and actions of &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;on Thursday 2 October 1952 and from which the scenario title is borrowed.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;In May 1945 the Royal Canadian Navy (RCN) was the third largest navy in the world&amp;nbsp;in numbers of commissioned combat ships.&amp;nbsp;The majority of these were frigates, corvettes and minesweepers whose primary role was as coastal and blue-water convoy escorts and the largest surface combatants were a pair of British-built light cruisers. After the massive post-war demobilization&amp;nbsp;a relative handful of destroyers constituted the core of the Fleet and at the top were the seven &lt;EM&gt;Tribal Class&lt;/EM&gt; ships each of more than 2500 tons.&amp;nbsp;Conceived&amp;nbsp;in the thirties&amp;nbsp;to defend the battle-line, a role that had ceased to be relevant, the &lt;EM&gt;Tribal's&lt;/EM&gt; served the RN, RAN and RCN effectively and with distinction performing duties probably never envisioned by their designers at the Admiralty. After the war, the Canadian ships&amp;nbsp;were taken in hand and&amp;nbsp;rearmed, with their older pattern twin 4.7" gun mounts replaced by&amp;nbsp;quick firing&amp;nbsp;4" high-angle, dual-purpose mounts and &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt; &lt;EM&gt;G-89&lt;/EM&gt; was amongst the first to be refitted.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Canada was quick to answer the call of the United Nations&amp;nbsp;to send&amp;nbsp;combat forces to Korea in 1950 although the Government was reluctant to commit forces from Europe or the Atlantic and as a result the naval mission fell to a handful of destroyers without any support units or infrastructure. &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Athabaskan R-79&lt;/EM&gt;, &lt;EM&gt;Cayuga R-04&lt;/EM&gt; and the &lt;EM&gt;V&lt;/EM&gt;-Class &lt;EM&gt;Sioux R-64&lt;/EM&gt; all arrived in&amp;nbsp;the Far East&amp;nbsp;by August&amp;nbsp;1950 and thereafter an RCN presence was constant through to 1955. Although the Army's specially mobilized brigade group was&amp;nbsp;incorporated&amp;nbsp;into the UN's Commonwealth Division, the Navy would be almost entirely dependent on the USN for all logistics and service support, which would be a new experience&amp;nbsp;and result in&amp;nbsp;more than a few growing pains. &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Iroquois &lt;/EM&gt;arrived in theatre in June 1952 for her first deployment where she operated with carrier task forces as an ASW escort, plane guard and in other general escort duties. In September 1952 she sailed to Korea's east coast where she was soon to join the gun-line and become a train-buster.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;You are Commanding Officer, Task Unit 95.22 in &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt; with &lt;EM&gt;USS Walker DD-517 USS Thompson DMS-38&lt;/EM&gt;, &lt;EM&gt;USS Carmick DMS-33&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;USS Marsh DE-699&lt;/EM&gt;. Your force&amp;nbsp;has relieved a Royal Navy task unit led by &lt;EM&gt;HMS Charity&lt;/EM&gt; that has spent several days ranging up and down the coast generally shelling North Korean military and logistics installations with special attention to the coastal rail lines. Train-busting is a welcome diversion from ASW escort and plane-guard duties and some of the targets in the coastal area between Songjin and Singpo were known as Package One through Package Five, each Package denoting one of the tunnels with the railroad targets strung out between them. It is now dawn, the skies are clear, the winds are calm and it's time to go train hunting. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;DIV class=MsoNormal style="TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt" align=center&gt;
&lt;HR align=center SIZE=2 width="100%"&gt;
&lt;/DIV&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Scenario Designer's Notes. What Really Happened:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;On the 1st&lt;EM&gt;, HMS Charity&lt;/EM&gt; had destroyed a train and shot up the track and when&amp;nbsp;enemy railroad repair crews showed up, her shells caused them to flee into the tunnel known as Package One and then kept them shut in, preventing them from clearing the track or affecting repairs. On the morning of the 2nd &lt;EM&gt;Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt; arrived with &lt;EM&gt;Marsh&lt;/EM&gt; and the latter was ordered to keep the tunnel entrance under fire while &lt;EM&gt;Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt; went to look for other targets. Returning around lunchtime in response to a request for support from &lt;EM&gt;Marsh&lt;/EM&gt;, which had come under fire of a pair of artillery batteries, &lt;EM&gt;Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt; closed the range to engage and at about 1600 hours was hit astern, suffering damage and fatalities. Laying smoke and zig-zagging, she kept up rapid and effective fire and after&amp;nbsp;hitting the&amp;nbsp;batteries, she withdrew with three dead and&amp;nbsp;eleven seriously&amp;nbsp;wounded. &lt;EM&gt;Marsh&lt;/EM&gt; had departed the action at high speed when&lt;EM&gt; Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;arrived and suffered no damage or casualties. Next day, after sending the most critical of the wounded to the tanker &lt;EM&gt;USS Chemung AO-30&lt;/EM&gt;, &lt;EM&gt;Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt; returned to Package One alone and pounded both shore batteries into silence. She stayed on the line until 15 October then went to Hong Kong for R&amp;amp;R and repairs.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The Scenario:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The account above is taken from &lt;EM&gt;Thunder in the Morning Calm&lt;/EM&gt;, which never really makes clear what the rest of Task Unit 95.22 was doing but has little good to say about the actions of &lt;EM&gt;USS Marsh&lt;/EM&gt;. Therefore the scenario is not designed to be entirely historical but rather sets up a similar situation to allow the Player to perhaps indulge in a little train busting and conduct their own bombardment program. There are some shore batteries, bridges, tunnels, buildings and locomotive targets. There may be mines and the two DMS with the task unit have been fitted with mine-hunting sonar and sweep gear. There may be some junks, civilian or perhaps not and maybe a surprise or two along the way. The Player has the entire coast between Songjin and Hongwong as an operational area and the scenario ends with the passing of daylight. There are some significant differences between the actual action and this scenario. Aircraft have been excluded although the force apparently had a spotter plane on hand to assist in correcting the fall of shot. There are probably more and varied targets available than there were in the event. Somewhere over the horizon is&amp;nbsp;the TF-95 CVBG ready to cover your force with a CAP should communist planes appear but as there will be none, the CVBG is not represented. There were two RoK Navy torpedo boats included with TU-95.22 but I left these out as irrelevant to the main bombardment mission.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;American's may well ask why&amp;nbsp;United States&amp;nbsp;ships are under command of&amp;nbsp;a "fern'ner" particularly as the USN so dominated the Korean theatre and the answer probably relates to interoperability (then a fairly new concept)&amp;nbsp;along with&amp;nbsp;naval rank protocols&amp;nbsp;and seniority. &lt;EM&gt;Iroquois&lt;/EM&gt;' captain was Commander W.M. Landymore, RCN and typically all of the &lt;EM&gt;Tribal&lt;/EM&gt; skippers at this time were substantive Commanders in rank. However, Cdr Landymore was also Canada's Senior Naval Officer in Korean waters&amp;nbsp;along with being&amp;nbsp;commander, Canadian Command: Destroyers Far East (CANCOMDESFE) and held an appointment as Acting Captain RCN, an&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;O6 equivalent.&amp;nbsp;&lt;EM&gt;Thompson&lt;/EM&gt;, &lt;EM&gt;Carmick&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;Marsh&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;were probably commanded by O4's (LCDR)&amp;nbsp;while &lt;EM&gt;Walker&lt;/EM&gt; was likely skippered by an O5 (CDR). Rather than cutting an O6 (CAPT) out of the available force pool&amp;nbsp;it would make sense to have made Cdr Landymore the officer in tactical command in what was intended to be a straightforward and entirely routine operation.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;You can still trace the coastal railroad using Google Earth and for the most part the bridges and rail yards and other&amp;nbsp;inviting targets today are assumed to correspond to 1952. Many of the potential targets are spotted and on the map as TU-95.22 was not the first bombardment group to visit this part of the DPRK coast. There are actually far more than five tunnels so what are possibly the Package tunnels have been&amp;nbsp;designated P1-P5 with north or south entrances identified but the excess tunnels have been given letter designations. Where several tunnels ran close together they have been combined in an attempt to maintain a bit of clarity in a target-rich environment. The railroad takes a significant jog inland leading to A-Tunnel, which exits near the sea again. Some of the railroad infrastructure may not be within range or line of sight for the Player and this is intentional.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Thanks for playing and good&amp;nbsp;luck&amp;nbsp;train busting.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
  <Complexity>2</Complexity>
  <Difficulty>3</Difficulty>
